WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.715
Hi and welcome back.

2
00:00:05.715 --> 00:00:09.195
In this course, you've learned
about object recognition,

3
00:00:09.195 --> 00:00:11.505
where the goal is
to input a picture

4
00:00:11.505 --> 00:00:14.010
and figure out what
is in the picture,

5
00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.930
such as is this a cat or not.

6
00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.075
You've learned about
object detection,

7
00:00:18.075 --> 00:00:20.190
where the goal is to further put

8
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.160
a bounding box around
the object is found.

9
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.460
In this video, you
learn about a set

10
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.385
of algorithms that's even
one step more sophisticated,

11
00:00:29.385 --> 00:00:31.800
which is semantic segmentation,

12
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.495
where the goal is to draw

13
00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:36.840
a careful outline around
the object that is

14
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.570
detected so that you
know exactly which

15
00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.825
pixels belong to the object
and which pixels don't.

16
00:00:42.825 --> 00:00:44.420
This type of algorithm,

17
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.060
semantic segmentation
is useful for

18
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.860
many commercial
applications as well

19
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.390
today. Let's dive in.

20
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.880
What is semantic segmentation?

21
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.350
Let's say you're building a
self-driving car and you see

22
00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.660
an input image like this and

23
00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.419
you'd like to detect the
position of the other cars.

24
00:01:02.419 --> 00:01:05.030
If you use an object
detection algorithm,

25
00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.620
the goal may be to draw

26
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.710
bounding boxes like these
around the other vehicles.

27
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.070
This might be good enough
for self-driving car,

28
00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.990
but if you want your
learning algorithm to figure

29
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.960
out what is every single
pixel in this image,

30
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.160
then you may use

31
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.560
a semantic segmentation algorithm

32
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.449
whose goal is to output this.

33
00:01:24.449 --> 00:01:27.730
Where, for example,
rather than detecting

34
00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.170
the road and trying to draw

35
00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.195
a bounding box around the roads,

36
00:01:31.195 --> 00:01:32.860
which isn't going
to be that useful,

37
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.640
with semantic segmentation
the algorithm attempts to

38
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.455
label every single pixel

39
00:01:38.455 --> 00:01:41.245
as is this drivable roads or not,

40
00:01:41.245 --> 00:01:43.850
indicated by the
dark green there.

41
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.275
One of the uses of
semantic segmentation

42
00:01:47.275 --> 00:01:51.100
is that it is used by some
self-driving car teams to

43
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.370
figure out exactly
which pixels are

44
00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.170
safe to drive over because they

45
00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.600
represent a drivable surface.

46
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.965
Let's look at some
other applications.

47
00:02:00.965 --> 00:02:03.970
These are a couple of images from

48
00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.310
research papers by Novikov
et al and by Dong et al.

49
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.170
In medical imaging,
given a chest X-ray,

50
00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.620
you may want to diagnose

51
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.465
if someone has a
certain condition,

52
00:02:14.465 --> 00:02:17.135
but what may be even
more helpful to doctors,

53
00:02:17.135 --> 00:02:19.565
is if you can segment
out in the image,

54
00:02:19.565 --> 00:02:22.220
exactly which pixels correspond

55
00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.310
to certain parts of
the patient's anatomy.

56
00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.650
In the image on the left,

57
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.720
the lungs, the heart,

58
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.480
and the clavicle, so

59
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.630
the collarbones are segmented
out using different colors.

60
00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.670
This segmentation can make it

61
00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.820
easier to spot
irregularities and diagnose

62
00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.760
serious diseases and also help

63
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.105
surgeons with planning
out surgeries.

64
00:02:47.105 --> 00:02:49.295
In this example,

65
00:02:49.295 --> 00:02:55.395
a brain MRI scan is used
for brain tumor detection.

66
00:02:55.395 --> 00:02:58.790
Manually segmenting
out this tumor

67
00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.730
is very time-consuming
and laborious,

68
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.474
but if a learning
algorithm can segment

69
00:03:04.474 --> 00:03:07.310
out the tumor automatically; this

70
00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.280
saves radiologists a lot
of time and this is

71
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.530
a useful input as well
for surgical planning.

72
00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.050
The algorithm used to generate

73
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.170
this result is an
algorithm called unit,

74
00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:22.165
which you'll learn about in
the remainder of this video.

75
00:03:22.165 --> 00:03:26.420
Let's dig into what semantic
segmentation actually does.

76
00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.130
For the sake of simplicity,

77
00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.365
let's use the example of

78
00:03:30.365 --> 00:03:34.225
segmenting out a car
from some background.

79
00:03:34.225 --> 00:03:37.250
Let's say for now that
the only thing you care

80
00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.700
about is segmenting out
the car in this image.

81
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.125
In that case, you may decide
to have two cause labels.

82
00:03:45.125 --> 00:03:50.045
One for a car and
zero for not car.

83
00:03:50.045 --> 00:03:52.085
In this case, the job of

84
00:03:52.085 --> 00:03:53.900
the segmentation algorithm of

85
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.870
the unit algorithm
will be to output,

86
00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.979
either one or zero for every
single pixel in this image,

87
00:04:00.979 --> 00:04:03.140
where a pixel should
be labeled one,

88
00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.210
if it is part of
the car and label

89
00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.730
zero if it's not part of the car.

90
00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:11.960
Alternatively, if you want
to segment this image,

91
00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.570
looking more finely
you may decide

92
00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:17.450
that you want to
label the car one.

93
00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.820
Maybe you also want to know
where the buildings are.

94
00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.195
In which case you would
have a second class,

95
00:04:22.195 --> 00:04:25.245
class two the building and then

96
00:04:25.245 --> 00:04:28.305
finally the ground or the roads,

97
00:04:28.305 --> 00:04:32.060
class three, in which case the
job the learning algorithm

98
00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:36.485
would be to label every
pixel as follows instead.

99
00:04:36.485 --> 00:04:40.640
Taking the per-pixel labels
and shifting it to the right,

100
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.160
this is the output

101
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:47.170
that we would like to train
a unit table to give.

102
00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.580
This is a lot of outputs,

103
00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.220
instead of just giving

104
00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.105
a single class label or
maybe a class label and

105
00:04:55.105 --> 00:04:57.770
coordinates needed to
specify bounding box

106
00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.730
the neural network
unit in this case,

107
00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:03.855
has to generate a whole
matrix of labels.

108
00:05:03.855 --> 00:05:07.675
What's the right neural network
architecture to do that?

109
00:05:07.675 --> 00:05:11.440
Let's start with the
object recognition

110
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.720
neural network
architecture that you're

111
00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.810
familiar with and let's
figure how to modify

112
00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.110
this in order to make
this new network output,

113
00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.535
a whole matrix of class labels.

114
00:05:22.535 --> 00:05:24.270
Here's a familiar

115
00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.040
convolutional neural
network architecture,

116
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.090
where you input an image
which is fed forward

117
00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:32.890
through multiple layers in order

118
00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:35.360
to generate a class label y hat.

119
00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.355
In order to change
this architecture into

120
00:05:38.355 --> 00:05:41.545
a semantic segmentation
architecture,

121
00:05:41.545 --> 00:05:44.050
let's get rid of the
last few layers and

122
00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:48.010
one key step of semantic
segmentation is that,

123
00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:51.700
whereas the dimensions
of the image have

124
00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.905
been generally getting smaller

125
00:05:53.905 --> 00:05:55.705
as we go from left to right,

126
00:05:55.705 --> 00:05:58.870
it now needs to get bigger
so they can gradually

127
00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.230
blow it back up to
a full-size image,

128
00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:04.955
which is a size you
want for the output.

129
00:06:04.955 --> 00:06:06.530
Specifically,

130
00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.310
this is what a unit
architecture looks like.

131
00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.835
As we go deeper into the unit,

132
00:06:11.835 --> 00:06:14.540
the height and width will go back

133
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.720
up while the number of
channels will decrease

134
00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.200
so the unit architecture
looks like this until

135
00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.120
eventually, you get your
segmentation map of the cat.

136
00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.560
One operation we have not yet

137
00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.365
covered is what does
this look like?

138
00:06:30.365 --> 00:06:32.780
To make the image bigger.

139
00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.390
To explain how that works,

140
00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:37.250
you have to know how to

141
00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.210
implement a transpose
convolution.

142
00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.430
That's semantic
segmentation, a very

143
00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.595
useful algorithm for many
computer vision applications

144
00:06:46.595 --> 00:06:48.710
where the key idea is you have to

145
00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:50.600
take every single pixel and

146
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:51.800
label every single pixel

147
00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.725
individually with the
appropriate class label.

148
00:06:54.725 --> 00:06:56.570
As you've seen in this video,

149
00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:59.750
a key step to do that is
to take a small set of

150
00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:01.490
activations and to blow it

151
00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:04.205
up to a bigger set
of activations.

152
00:07:04.205 --> 00:07:05.555
In order to do that,

153
00:07:05.555 --> 00:07:06.950
you have to implement something

154
00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:09.050
called the transpose convolution,

155
00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.360
which is important
operation that is used

156
00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.215
multiple times in the
unit architecture.

157
00:07:14.215 --> 00:07:16.790
Let's go on to the next
video where you learn

158
00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:19.205
about the transpose convolution.

159
00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:21.540
I'll see you in the next video.